the cell  so therapeutic neutralizing antibodies  you know also target this region a spike  protein  and there's somewhere on the order of  about 20 different monoclonal antibodies  um that are that are currently in  clinical trials  as treatment options for the infection  with coronavirus and there are many more  that are in pre-clinical development as  well  so although mutation rates of the virus  are slow  some changes in the receptor binding  domain have been seen in some isolates  and it remains important to monitor uh  the virus for changes um of sequence in  the receptor binding domain  sumane is about 183 amino acids and so  it can easily be sequenced by  sanger sequencing and there's no need to  sequence the entire virus for this  purpose  so after the virus has entered the cell  newly formed virus is ingested by  antigen presenting cells that's shown in  the diagram which go on  to display fragments of the virus and  can activate  t helper cells which then go on to turn  on b  cells which produce um antibodies  um against the virus or to activate  cytotoxic t cells which can destroy the  virus  um so b cells can be isolated from  patients infected with the virus by  using the  spike protein as bait  and then a technique like flow cytometry  to isolate  individual b cells and there are  well-established protocols for saying a  sequencing of heavy chain  and light chain regions of antibodies  and that have been recovered from b  cells so this is really useful for  identifying type binding antibody  sequences  um  finally as we begin to think ahead to  the prospect  of an effective coronavirus vaccine next  year  the question of durability of response  in an individual  is an important question um and there's  a growing  body of research that links immune  response to  variations in the microbiome composition  present in our bodies so  there are many there are as many as 100  times more bacterial genes residing in  humans than  human genes and recent data indicates  that gut microbiota can modulate the  response to vaccines  and as we'll see in the next few slides  a fragment analysis basic  approach can provide a rapid  cost-effective approach to  profiling microbiota in the gut and the  airways  so a technique uh known as iso  is pro has been developed by a small  startup company in the netherlands  called in biome  um and their approach is based on the  specific amplification of interspace  regions in bacteria  to generate fluorescently labeled  fragments that can then be separated  on applied biosystems genetic analyzer  instruments so all bacteria  have at least one in the space region  and men and many bacteria have multiple  regions  and is pro relies on length and sequence  polymorphisms  of these interspace regions to provide  identification of the bacteria  so for example in the bottom of this  slide show a  these interspace regions for this  particular  bacterial species enterococcus basalis  it has four different interspace regions  is one is two ice iii and is4  when uh after amplification of is1 and  is2 you get a  377 base base pair fragment  um and amplification of is3 and is4  produces  275 base pair fragment and so each  bacteria has  an almost unique fingerprint uh when  these interspace regions  um are amplified and you can also  group different phylum of bacteria um  in by using different labeled  primers so uh three different colors  shown here for three different file  and some of the advantages of this  approach um  are that it's a rapid workflow you can  get an answer within about a four to  five hour turnaround time  does not require culture of the bacteria  and it can detect unknown and unknown  species  no need to predefine the species that  you're looking for  um and in addition um it  can detect bacterial bacteria that  cannot be cultivated so our colleagues  in biome have used this approach to  examine  microbiota and nasal pharyngeal swabs  collected from patients the  individuals that have been infected with  osiris-cov2  and they have used this technique to  identify  specific microbiota profiles that may be  correlated to the likelihood of  infection of the virus  they examined 135 different clinical  research samples from people ranging  in age from the very young to the very  elderly in this zero to 100 age  range and they  use the ios pro assay to profile dna  isolated from these nasopharyngeal fraud  so what you can see  on the slide is a resulting claustrogram  where the pcr status of each individual  is listed either as red or green red  being positive for the virus green being  negative for the virus  and these were independently determined  by qpcr  um and then you can you can see that um  after unsupervised clustering was  performed of all of the profiles there's  a clear  profile of the 77 samples with similar  microbiota uh profile in the center of  the  uh the cluster gram here um  and in addition  well um so this cluster of  um this central cluster showed a  very low level or a relatively low level  of co2 positivity compared to the  cluster  that was outside of the um  of the central cluster so in the for the  cluster about 25 percent of the  people were cov2 a positive and outside  of this cluster this rose to 47 percent  and in addition um  data that's not shown in the slide  indicates that older individuals were  actually enriched for this  h signature which poses the question  whether there is an age-dependent  pharyngeal microbiota signature  that has an increased susceptibility so  further and more detailed analysis that  this data set can be  found in this publication that's listed  on the slide  or alternatively there is a poster that  will also be presented in this  conference that  goes into more detail into the data  analysis and the conclusions  so i want to switch gears now and talk  a little bit about the use of sanger  let's do an article um because wide  recognition demonic disease is a growing  threat in fact the cdc estimates that  more than  every 10 people can be spread from  animals  and in fact three out of four new or  emerging infectious diseases actually  uh animals into people and they can we  can pass  um either directly um from an animal  into a human for example if we discuss  um andromedas uh we're responsible  for directly transferring the mers virus  um and of course we have a bird flu and  swine flu that can be directly  transferred into humans  or um they can start from a viral source  such as a bat and pass through  um another animal either a civet as in  the case of  original sars or it's postulated that  the pangolins are the intermediary  uh hosts for the sarcov2 virus  so there's a growing need for assays  that can detect pathogens  that can pass from humans to animals to  humans  and also to identify the natural  reservoir for a particular pathogen that  has  the possibility of infecting human  population  so sequencing can be used to address  this  to provide information on pathogens in  animal and animal hosts that are  closely related to humanized versions  and more likely to cross the  species boundary so one  approach that has been used for this  purpose  that is has capability to  identify all currently recognized and  potentially future  emergent coronaviruses is an rt-pcr  amplification based approach  followed by sequencing of a particular  region of the coronavirus this  rna-dependent  rna polymerase region of the virus in  the orth  b region and this approach is described  in the publication listed at the top of  the slide here in the journal of  clinical  virology but has been widely used by  others as well  the approach consists of two different  pcrs that use degenerate primers  a first pcr that produces  an amplicon about 670 bases in length  with degenerate forward and reverse  primers and then a second pcr  that is a nested approach that generates  a smaller fragment  again using a degenerate primer of about  600 base pairs and this  amplicon can then be sanger sequenced  to confirm the identity so with this  approach you know there are a large  number of coronaviruses that can be  identified  um and the assay shows uh  virtually no cross-reactivity with other  common respiratory pathogens such as  rsb or flu and it can be used  to routinely screen uh individuals  whose occupation bring them into close  contact with animals  for prolonged periods of time or it can  be used for  routine surveillance in species such as  bats  which are reservoirs for viruses with  human pathogenic potential  so finally i'd like to briefly describe  an application  of ce and the study of sara cov to  detection that is based on a fragment  analysis approach  the workflow for ce fragment analysis  detection is actually similar to qpcr it  starts with a collection of the sample  in a via nasopharyngeal swab and then  extraction  of the rna for example using  one of the kits from thermo fissure a  magmax viral pathogen nucleic acid  isolation kit and then after rna  extraction  an rt pcr step is performed with  fluorescently  labeled primers and at the end of this  the  fragments recently labeled fragments  that have been amplified  are identified on the ce  and the size of the fragment and the  color of the fragment  encodes data about which amplicon is  being detected  and the data can be analyzed via a  software such as a  g-matter from thermo-fisher so for the  assay developed at thermo-fissure there  were three different regions of the  virus that were amplified  one from the off 1ab region the second  from the spike  gene and the third from the nuclear  capsule gene and this results in three  different size fragments  an n protein fragment at 396 base pairs  and off 1ab  425 and s protein at 437 base pairs  and additionally an rna positive control  is added  to the reaction and to help check for  the presence of  any uh pcr inhibitors that might prevent  amplification  so one added feature of this assay that  i'd like to point out  is the use of multiple fluorescently  labeled primers  this allows after amplification for the  samples to be combined to be pooled  together  such that four individual reactions can  be run  on the capillary combined and read out  and run on the ce  and the fragments are then identified  based on not only the size but also the  color so  each capillary can then produce answers  for for the application from four  individuals  and the sensitivity of this approach is  is similar to qpcr methods  and more details on the protocol  and an application note can be found on  the thermofisher website as shown on the  slide  including the sequences of all of the  primers  that are used in the assay  so um by way of summary um  what i've hopefully what i've shown you  is that sanger sequencing and fragment  analysis technologies  are widely useful in the detection and  sequencing of sars cov2  sangro sequencing can be used to support  an ngs  whole genome sequencing by a gap filling  and confirmation of variants but can  also be used  to generate a whole genome sequence  by itself targeted sequencing you know  can be used  to look at cyber cov2 host response  factors such as  a sequence of h2 receptors  which the virus binds to to gain entry  to the cell or can be used to  look at the immune response  look at the antibody sequences that are  produced from individual  b cells by sequencing of the variable  regions  um there has been an assay developed  that amplifies and sequences a highly  conserved region  of the virus the rdpr rprp region  and this can be useful to identify the  natural reservoir of the virus or to  help track  animal coronaviruses that may jump to  species  and then finally bacterial profiling  using the is pro technology  can develop a more comprehensive  understanding of how the microbiome can  influence host response to pathogen  infection  so with that i i'll conclude my talk and  invite any questions and thank you for  your time  [Music]  [Music]  hmm  [Music]  sorry i believe you couldn't hear me  that's yes i can hear you now thank you  my bad thank you so i was just saying  thank you dr williams for your  outstanding presentation and we'll now  move into the live  q a portion of this presentation um and  as a reminder please submit your  questions  to the q a box so our first question  again  it says sorry i may have missed this in  your talk when would i use stranger  sequencing as the primary sequencing  method  for sars cov2 yeah great question thanks  for that  i think there are a couple of  circumstances under which you might  consider using sanger sequencing  um obviously you know it's not suitable  for sequencing large numbers of virus  or viral samples but um for individual  viral samples  it is possibility since the genome is 30  000 bases and that's accessible  uh via the sanger method it takes about  you know about a day  to sequence a single virus and that  obviously scales linearly so if you need  to sequence more  it will take corresponding number of  days  but for single samples i think sanger is  an option i think the second place  where it's an option is for low viral  load  sanger is a quite a sensitive technique  um it's possible to go down  to um you know sort of 10 copy range  and obtain good sequencing and data  and of course ngs does struggle a little  bit  at the low copy number in getting a  really good coverage  in fact recently i saw a publication  where they had used sanger sequencing  for sequencing the virus  that had been isolated from the air  ducts in in hospitals  and in that case the the viral the  levels of the virus were very low and  sanger sequencing  uh proved very effective um in  determining  the uh the complete genome sequence of  the virus in that case so  for single samples sanger is useful and  then also for  samples that have low viral load i think  those are the two primary usages  wonderful and i do have one more  question for you if we can ans